//  vertical column whose axis is CUMULATIVE HOURS — 0 H on the floor, a 60 H
//  ceiling line capping the top — filled bottom-up by one stratum per day of
//  the rolling seven-day window, oldest stratum at the bottom, so the escort
//  reads the total, the days that built it, and which day rolls out next, in a
//  single glance.
//
//  Anti-clone: deliberately NOT ES-24 Hazmat Watch, the other escort COMPLIANCE
//  instrument panel and the nearest sibling. ES-24's organ is a DECAY TRACK: a
//  HORIZONTAL 0-to-40-minute age ruler that DEPLETES left-to-right toward a
//  STATUTORY 30-minute staleness boundary (49 CFR §177.817). ES-28 reads in the
//  opposite direction on every axis they share — VERTICAL, ACCUMULATING upward
//  rather than depleting, and bounded by a HOUSE ceiling rather than a statute,
//  because escorts are HOS-EXEMPT (ROLES.ESCORT is absent from hosReadProcedure,
//  hos.ts:28-39) and this surface says so on its face instead of borrowing a
//  legal boundary it does not have. ES-24 asks how old one number is; ES-28
//  asks how much has piled up and whether the pile can be trusted. No decay
//  track, no age ruler, no wind rose, no statutory line here.
//  Also deliberately NOT ES-09 Escort Home's 16-HOUR DAY-RULER — a horizontal
//  time-of-day axis (05..21) with one block laid on it. ES-28 has no
//  time-of-day axis at all: a day here is a BAND WITH A HEIGHT, not a position
//  on a clock, and seven of them stack. Also NOT ES-15 Schedule Availability's
//  6x7 CALENDAR LATTICE — no lattice, no date cells, no capsule bands; seven
//  days are seven strata in one column, ordered by age.
